answer.text:
Assuming both pMOS transistors are in saturation, the bias voltages are ideal DC sources, and body effect is neglected:

- For output-resistance analysis, \(V_{DD},V_{b1},V_{b2}\) are AC grounds.
- Since \(v_{gs2}=0\), \(M_2\) contributes only its drain resistance \(r_{o2}\) at node \(X\).
- Thus \(M_1\) has source degeneration \(R_S=r_{o2}\).

Applying a test voltage \(v_t\) at the output and denoting the voltage at \(X\) by \(v_x\),

\[
(g_{m1}+g_{o1}+g_{o2})v_x=g_{o1}v_t,
\qquad
i_t=g_{o2}v_x,
\]

where \(g_{oi}=1/r_{oi}\). Therefore,

\[
\boxed{
R_{\text{out}}
=\frac{g_{m1}+g_{o1}+g_{o2}}{g_{o1}g_{o2}}
}
\]

or equivalently,

\[
\boxed{
R_{\text{out}}
=r_{o1}+r_{o2}+g_{m1}r_{o1}r_{o2}
=r_{o1}+r_{o2}(1+g_{m1}r_{o1})
}
\]

If body effect in \(M_1\) is included, replace \(g_{m1}\) by the body-effect-adjusted transconductance (e.g. \(g_{m1}+g_{mb1}\), depending on convention).

Assume the small-signal model with \(r_o\to\infty\), ideal \(I_{SS}\), and \(V_b\) as an AC ground. Let \(v_i=v_P\).

The open-loop gain from \(P\) to \(V_{\text{out}}\) is

\[
A_{OL}=g_{m1}g_{m2}R_{D1}R_{D2},
\]

since

\[
v_X=-g_{m1}R_{D1}v_i,
\qquad
v_{\text{out}}=-g_{m2}R_{D2}v_X=A_{OL}v_i.
\]

With \(R_F\) connected, the output-node equation is

\[
v_{\text{out}}
=A_{OL}v_i+R_{D2}\frac{v_i-v_{\text{out}}}{R_F},
\]

so

\[
\frac{v_{\text{out}}}{v_i}
=\frac{A_{OL}R_F+R_{D2}}{R_F+R_{D2}}.
\]

The input current is

\[
i_{\text{in}}
=sC_{\text{in}}v_i+\frac{v_i-v_{\text{out}}}{R_F}.
\]

Therefore,

\[
\boxed{
Z_{\text{in}}(s)
=
\left[
sC_{\text{in}}
+\frac{1-g_{m1}g_{m2}R_{D1}R_{D2}}
       {R_F+R_{D2}}
\right]^{-1}
}
\]

or equivalently,

\[
\boxed{
Z_{\text{in}}(s)
=
\frac{R_F+R_{D2}}
{1-g_{m1}g_{m2}R_{D1}R_{D2}
+sC_{\text{in}}(R_F+R_{D2})}
}.
\]

At DC,

\[
\boxed{
R_{\text{in}}
=
\frac{R_F+R_{D2}}
{1-g_{m1}g_{m2}R_{D1}R_{D2}}
}.
\]

Thus the input resistance is positive if \(g_{m1}g_{m2}R_{D1}R_{D2}<1\), infinite for unity loop gain, and negative if the loop gain exceeds unity.
